<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">CloudTrail</data>
      <data key="d6">CloudTrail</data>
      <data key="d7">AWS public-cloud telemetry source, expanded from the AWS cloud icon. Events are pulled into Apache Metron through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">GuardDuty</data>
      <data key="d6">GuardDuty</data>
      <data key="d7">AWS public-cloud telemetry source, expanded from the AWS cloud icon. Findings are pulled into Metron through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">S3 (AWS log sources)</data>
      <data key="d6">S3</data>
      <data key="d7">AWS public-cloud telemetry source, expanded from the AWS cloud icon. This is separate from the Security Data Lake S3. Logs are pulled through Apache NiFi.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">SNS</data>
      <data key="d6">SNS</data>
      <data key="d7">AWS public-cloud telemetry source, expanded from the AWS cloud icon. Integrated through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">SQS</data>
      <data key="d6">SQS</data>
      <data key="d7">AWS public-cloud telemetry source, expanded from the AWS cloud icon. Integrated through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">On-Prem Data Sources</data>
      <data key="d6">ThirdParty</data>
      <data key="d7">On-premise telemetry sources. Events are pulled directly into Apache Metron using standard syslog or Kafka integration.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6">
      <data key="d5">Apache Metron</data>
      <data key="d6">ThirdParty</data>
      <data key="d7">The core of the next-gen SOC (stream-processing layer, contributed by Hortonworks). Parsing turns syslog, CloudTrail and GuardDuty logs into a common JSON format. Enrichment adds context such as source IP geolocation, AS number and destination server role (flight, hotel or payment server). Profiling covers only a limited time window, depending on infrastructure. HDFS is the persistence backbone. About 20 sources, about 5,000 events per second.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="7">
      <data key="d5">EMR</data>
      <data key="d6">EMR</data>
      <data key="d7">Part of the Security Data Lake. An EMR job picks up persistent data from Metron's HDFS and transforms it into S3. This replaced the earlier on-prem HDFS indexing setup.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="8">
      <data key="d5">S3 (Security Data Lake)</data>
      <data key="d6">S3</data>
      <data key="d7">Serverless, horizontally scalable store for telemetry events, kept for months or years. About 250 million events indexed per day.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="9">
      <data key="d5">Athena</data>
      <data key="d6">Athena</data>
      <data key="d7">Runs SQL queries and analytics on S3 data for log mining, analytics and forensics. Generates long-term models and profiles that feed back into Metron. Planned use: threat hunting.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="10">
      <data key="d5">SOAR (Blitz)</data>
      <data key="d6">ThirdParty</data>
      <data key="d7">Security orchestration, automation and response framework called Blitz, open-sourced on GitHub. Takes alerts from Metron, adds context such as reverse DNS and WHOIS lookups, and presents everything in a single pane of glass (mobile or Jira integration) to cut triage time.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="11">
      <data key="d5">SOC Analysts (Alerts)</data>
      <data key="d6">UserCompanyAnalyst</data>
      <data key="d7">SOC analysts receive enriched alerts on mobile or Jira and can respond with one click, for example by blocking a source.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="12">
      <data key="d5">WAF</data>
      <data key="d6">WAF</data>
      <data key="d7">Mitigation target for semi-automated incident response, for example blocking a source IP. Firewalls and internal routers are also integrated.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">CloudTrail telemetry events are pulled into Apache Metron through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">GuardDuty telemetry events are pulled into Apache Metron through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
      <data key="d10">0'</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">S3 log telemetry is pulled into Apache Metron through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
      <data key="d10">0''</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">SNS telemetry is pulled into Apache Metron through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
      <data key="d10">0'''</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">SQS telemetry is pulled into Apache Metron through Apache NiFi workflows.</data>
      <data key="d10">0''''</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">On-prem events are ingested directly through syslog or Kafka integration.</data>
      <data key="d10">0'''''</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">An EMR job picks up persistent data from Metron's HDFS.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6" target="10" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">Metron sends detected alerts or incidents to the Blitz SOAR.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="7" target="8" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">EMR transforms the HDFS persistent data and writes it to S3.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="8" target="9" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Athena runs SQL queries over long-term S3 telemetry for log mining, analytics and forensics.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="9" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Feedback loop: models and profiles built from months of data-lake telemetry go back to Metron. These are more accurate than short-window stream profiles.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">meta</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="10" target="11" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">SOAR enriches the alert (reverse DNS, WHOIS) and presents it in a single pane of glass on mobile or Jira.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="11" target="12" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">The analyst triggers mitigation with one click, for example blocking the source on the WAF.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">MakeMyTrip Next-Gen SOC with Apache Metron and Serverless Security Data Lake</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-3lnf5lzsH0</data>
    <data key="d2">MakeMyTrip built a next-gen SOC from the ground up. It detects abnormal behavior beyond signature or rule-based detection, for example compromised servers that skew their Producer-Consumer Ratio (PCR). The system covers hybrid ingestion from AWS and on-prem, Apache Metron as the stream-processing core (parsing, enrichment and profiling), a serverless Security Data Lake (EMR -&gt; S3 -&gt; Athena) that feeds profiles back to Metron, and the Blitz SOAR for semi-automated response through WAF, firewalls and routers. Scale: about 20 data sources, about 5,000 events per second, and about 250 million events per day indexed in the data lake. Retention runs to months or years. Next step: threat hunting on the data lake.</data>
    <data key="d3">Security, SOC, SIEM, Data Lake, Analytics, Incident Response</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
